1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:07,739
In 2010, one of the most powerful
names in smartphones wasn't Apple.

2
00:00:08,206 --> 00:00:09,373
It was BlackBerry.

3
00:00:09,855 --> 00:00:14,851
In the United States, BlackBerry accounted
for roughly forty-three percent of the

4
00:00:14,891 --> 00:00:17,921
smartphone installed base at
the beginning of that year.

5
00:00:18,342 --> 00:00:23,399
Its devices were trusted by businesses,
governments, and millions of people who

6
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:24,989
needed to stay connected.

7
00:00:25,417 --> 00:00:30,487
But within just three years, its American
market share would collapse into single

8
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:31,527
digits.

9
00:00:31,856 --> 00:00:36,392
How did a company that helped define the
smartphone become one of its most famous

10
00:00:36,432 --> 00:00:37,432
casualties?

11
00:00:39,072 --> 00:00:41,191
September twenty-eighth, 2016.

12
00:00:41,823 --> 00:00:46,166
BlackBerry announced it would end all
internal smartphone hardware development.

13
00:00:47,166 --> 00:00:50,484
Future hardware efforts would
be outsourced to partners.

14
00:00:51,061 --> 00:00:55,678
For a company once synonymous with the mobile
phone, the decision was extraordinary.

15
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:58,579
BlackBerry wasn't going bankrupt.

16
00:00:59,107 --> 00:01:04,573
It was redirecting its business toward
software, security, and enterprise services.

17
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:08,774
But the announcement confirmed something
the market had already decided.

18
00:01:09,393 --> 00:01:13,324
The company that had made mobile email
essential could no longer compete

19
00:01:13,364 --> 00:01:16,394
successfully as a major
smartphone manufacturer.

20
00:01:17,117 --> 00:01:19,823
The real story began decades earlier.

21
00:01:21,556 --> 00:01:28,147
In 1984, Mike Lazaridis and Douglas Fregin
founded Research In Motion in Waterloo,

22
00:01:28,337 --> 00:01:29,163
Canada.

23
00:01:29,203 --> 00:01:32,453
The company specialized
in wireless technology.

24
00:01:32,845 --> 00:01:37,478
In the late nineteen-nineties, it began
introducing devices that brought digital

25
00:01:37,518 --> 00:01:39,868
communication beyond the office.

26
00:01:40,403 --> 00:01:44,673
The original BlackBerry
solution launched in 1999.

27
00:01:45,182 --> 00:01:47,238
Its central promise was powerful.

28
00:01:47,812 --> 00:01:51,741
You no longer needed to sit at a
desk to receive important messages.

29
00:01:52,410 --> 00:01:55,627
For businesses, that changed
the meaning of availability.

30
00:01:56,326 --> 00:02:01,825
For Research In Motion, it created the
foundation of a global technology empire.

31
00:02:03,632 --> 00:02:06,972
BlackBerry built its reputation
around three strengths.

32
00:02:07,569 --> 00:02:09,292
Reliable mobile messaging.

33
00:02:09,858 --> 00:02:11,165
Enterprise security.

34
00:02:11,775 --> 00:02:16,878
And physical keyboards that made
writing emails unusually practical.

35
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:21,616
BlackBerry Messenger added another
reason to stay inside its ecosystem.

36
00:02:22,776 --> 00:02:25,265
Users developed habits around the devices.

37
00:02:25,996 --> 00:02:28,617
Organizations built systems around them.

38
00:02:28,978 --> 00:02:33,562
And switching phones could mean
abandoning a familiar way of working.

39
00:02:33,983 --> 00:02:36,993
The company's products weren't
merely communication tools.

40
00:02:37,580 --> 00:02:41,191
They had become part of the daily
operating system of business.

41
00:02:41,838 --> 00:02:44,349
That loyalty would help BlackBerry grow.

42
00:02:44,837 --> 00:02:48,323
It would also complicate
its response to change.

43
00:02:50,493 --> 00:02:55,140
On January ninth, 2007,
Apple introduced the iPhone.

44
00:02:55,692 --> 00:02:57,225
It wasn't the first smartphone.

45
00:02:57,835 --> 00:03:00,158
And BlackBerry didn't
immediately disappear.

46
00:03:00,783 --> 00:03:05,252
In fact, Research In Motion
continued growing for several years.

47
00:03:05,710 --> 00:03:09,158
But the iPhone emphasized a
different product experience.

48
00:03:09,875 --> 00:03:11,077
A large touchscreen.

49
00:03:11,773 --> 00:03:14,294
A browser designed for
the mobile internet.

50
00:03:14,829 --> 00:03:18,526
And software built around
direct interaction.

51
00:03:18,993 --> 00:03:22,220
BlackBerry had optimized the
smartphone for messaging.

52
00:03:22,693 --> 00:03:26,583
Apple was helping redefine it as a
general-purpose pocket computer.

53
00:03:27,088 --> 00:03:29,567
The difference would become
increasingly important.

54
00:03:31,364 --> 00:03:34,438
In 2008, Apple launched the App Store.

55
00:03:34,906 --> 00:03:37,775
Android devices also began
entering the market.

56
00:03:38,257 --> 00:03:41,748
The smartphone battle was
becoming a platform war.

57
00:03:42,181 --> 00:03:43,626
Applications mattered.

58
00:03:44,166 --> 00:03:45,397
Developers mattered.

59
00:03:45,952 --> 00:03:50,902
And the operating system increasingly
determined the value of the device.

60
00:03:51,558 --> 00:03:54,636
BlackBerry had its own
applications and services.

61
00:03:55,275 --> 00:03:59,445
But Apple and Android began building
broader consumer ecosystems.

62
00:04:00,314 --> 00:04:04,766
The phone was no longer primarily
a secure communications device.

63
00:04:05,407 --> 00:04:10,387
It was becoming a camera, entertainment
system, navigation tool, shopping platform,

64
00:04:10,577 --> 00:04:11,577
and workplace.

65
00:04:12,047 --> 00:04:13,090
All in one.

66
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:19,008
BlackBerry's physical keyboard was one
of its greatest competitive advantages.

67
00:04:19,595 --> 00:04:24,541
But a strength can become a constraint
when customer expectations change.

68
00:04:25,101 --> 00:04:28,365
Larger touchscreens gave
applications more room.

69
00:04:28,809 --> 00:04:31,840
They supported new
interfaces and experiences.

70
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:37,881
BlackBerry experimented with touchscreen
devices and hybrid designs, including the

71
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:39,651
Storm and Torch families.

72
00:04:40,241 --> 00:04:45,491
So it would be inaccurate to say the company
refused to build touchscreen phones.

73
00:04:46,069 --> 00:04:47,689
The problem was deeper.

74
00:04:48,181 --> 00:04:52,743
BlackBerry struggled to move from a
keyboard-centered product philosophy toward a

75
00:04:52,783 --> 00:04:55,413
competitive, software-first platform.

76
00:04:55,937 --> 00:04:57,630
And the market wasn't waiting.

77
00:04:59,344 --> 00:05:03,013
The numbers revealed how quickly
the balance was changing.

78
00:05:03,510 --> 00:05:09,259
In December 2009, BlackBerry represented
approximately forty-two percent of the

79
00:05:09,299 --> 00:05:11,869
United States smartphone installed base.

80
00:05:12,437 --> 00:05:16,933
By September 2011, that figure had
fallen to roughly nineteen percent.

81
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:21,423
By September 2013, it
was around four percent.

82
00:05:21,751 --> 00:05:26,722
During the same period, Apple and
Android gained enormous ground.

83
00:05:27,185 --> 00:05:29,548
These were not global shipment figures.

84
00:05:30,103 --> 00:05:33,311
They measured the American
smartphone installed base.

85
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:36,022
But the trend was unmistakable.

86
00:05:36,503 --> 00:05:41,209
BlackBerry was losing the market where
it had once been exceptionally strong.

87
00:05:42,854 --> 00:05:48,191
In 2011, Research In Motion introduced
the BlackBerry PlayBook tablet.

88
00:05:48,555 --> 00:05:54,771
It used a new operating-system foundation
based on technology acquired through QNX.

89
00:05:55,551 --> 00:06:00,101
The device demonstrated that the company
was investing in a more modern software

90
00:06:00,141 --> 00:06:01,141
architecture.

91
00:06:01,523 --> 00:06:03,146
But the launch was troubled.

92
00:06:03,703 --> 00:06:08,754
The PlayBook initially lacked native email,
calendar, and contacts applications,

93
00:06:09,004 --> 00:06:13,554
requiring integration with a BlackBerry
smartphone for some familiar functions.

94
00:06:14,132 --> 00:06:19,009
For a company whose reputation had been
built on messaging, the omissions were

95
00:06:19,049 --> 00:06:20,339
especially damaging.

96
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:24,030
BlackBerry was trying to
reinvent its software.

97
00:06:24,583 --> 00:06:26,634
The execution was proving difficult.

98
00:06:28,435 --> 00:06:29,983
Then came another blow.

99
00:06:30,448 --> 00:06:35,704
In October 2011, BlackBerry suffered a
major service outage affecting customers

100
00:06:35,744 --> 00:06:37,534
across multiple continents.

101
00:06:38,192 --> 00:06:41,371
Email, messaging, and internet
services were disrupted.

102
00:06:41,963 --> 00:06:46,913
The failure began in its network infrastructure,
and backup systems did not work as

103
00:06:46,953 --> 00:06:47,953
intended.

104
00:06:48,423 --> 00:06:53,101
For a company whose brand depended on
dependable communication, the incident was

105
00:06:53,141 --> 00:06:54,571
particularly painful.

106
00:06:55,059 --> 00:06:58,862
The outage didn't cause
BlackBerry's decline by itself.

107
00:06:59,417 --> 00:07:04,002
But it exposed the risks of a tightly
managed communications ecosystem.

108
00:07:04,791 --> 00:07:07,983
And it arrived while competitors
were gaining ground.

109
00:07:09,810 --> 00:07:15,841
On January thirtieth, 2013, Research
In Motion introduced BlackBerry 10.

110
00:07:16,305 --> 00:07:19,676
The company also adopted
BlackBerry as its corporate name.

111
00:07:20,121 --> 00:07:25,827
Its new smartphones included the
touchscreen Z10 and keyboard-equipped Q10.

112
00:07:26,232 --> 00:07:28,819
This was not a company
refusing to innovate.

113
00:07:29,366 --> 00:07:33,871
BlackBerry had developed a substantially
redesigned operating system with new

114
00:07:33,911 --> 00:07:37,941
gestures, multitasking
features, and messaging tools.

115
00:07:38,528 --> 00:07:44,022
But the platform arrived after years
of rapid growth by iOS and Android.

116
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:47,957
The challenge was no longer simply
building a better BlackBerry.

117
00:07:48,448 --> 00:07:53,423
It was convincing users and developers
to invest in another ecosystem.

118
00:07:55,461 --> 00:08:00,668
By 2013, Apple and Android had
established powerful network effects.

119
00:08:01,261 --> 00:08:04,710
Developers prioritized
platforms with large audiences.

120
00:08:05,368 --> 00:08:09,708
Customers preferred platforms offering
the applications they wanted.

121
00:08:09,748 --> 00:08:12,671
And those preferences
reinforced each other.

122
00:08:13,042 --> 00:08:15,334
BlackBerry faced a difficult cycle.

123
00:08:15,789 --> 00:08:18,617
It needed applications to attract users.

124
00:08:19,143 --> 00:08:21,839
But it needed users to attract developers.

125
00:08:22,596 --> 00:08:28,006
Even strong hardware and a redesigned
operating system could not instantly solve

126
00:08:28,046 --> 00:08:29,046
that problem.

127
00:08:29,389 --> 00:08:32,584
BlackBerry had been early
to mobile communication.

128
00:08:32,628 --> 00:08:36,858
But in the new platform economy, it
was struggling to regain momentum.

129
00:08:38,775 --> 00:08:41,885
BlackBerry's financial statements
reflected the pressure.

130
00:08:42,432 --> 00:08:48,339
Its fiscal 2014 reporting documented declining
handset demand, intense competition,

131
00:08:48,789 --> 00:08:50,819
and substantial financial losses.

132
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:55,847
The company recorded significant
inventory-related charges as its product strategy

133
00:08:55,887 --> 00:08:57,237
struggled.

134
00:08:57,702 --> 00:09:02,294
BlackBerry 10 had not delivered the
recovery management had hoped for.

135
00:09:02,666 --> 00:09:07,737
The company still possessed valuable
technology, patents, enterprise relationships,

136
00:09:08,047 --> 00:09:09,657
and security expertise.

137
00:09:10,286 --> 00:09:14,575
But the handset business was no longer
the engine of growth it had once been.

138
00:09:15,047 --> 00:09:17,250
The company needed a different future.

139
00:09:19,085 --> 00:09:23,943
In November 2013, John Chen became
BlackBerry's chief executive.

140
00:09:24,603 --> 00:09:29,477
He accelerated a shift toward enterprise
software, security, and services.

141
00:09:30,457 --> 00:09:34,149
BlackBerry also experimented
with Android-powered devices.

142
00:09:34,942 --> 00:09:39,261
But in September 2016, the company announced
it would stop developing smartphone

143
00:09:39,301 --> 00:09:40,831
hardware internally.

144
00:09:41,385 --> 00:09:46,209
Future branded devices could still be
produced through licensing and manufacturing

145
00:09:46,249 --> 00:09:47,249
partnerships.

146
00:09:47,911 --> 00:09:49,318
The distinction matters.

147
00:09:49,978 --> 00:09:54,005
BlackBerry did not instantly abandon
every phone bearing its name.

148
00:09:54,366 --> 00:09:58,422
It abandoned the model of competing as a
vertically integrated smartphone hardware

149
00:09:58,462 --> 00:09:59,572
manufacturer.

150
00:10:01,299 --> 00:10:04,365
So what caused BlackBerry's
collapse in smartphones?

151
00:10:05,287 --> 00:10:06,682
Not simply the iPhone.

152
00:10:07,273 --> 00:10:08,730
And not simply the keyboard.

153
00:10:09,348 --> 00:10:12,053
The evidence points to a
combination of problems.

154
00:10:12,674 --> 00:10:16,508
A product identity centered on messaging
while the market expanded toward

155
00:10:16,548 --> 00:10:17,548
applications.

156
00:10:18,297 --> 00:10:21,224
Difficulty executing a
modern software transition.

157
00:10:21,857 --> 00:10:24,552
A growing disadvantage
in developer ecosystems.

158
00:10:25,529 --> 00:10:29,797
And a delayed response to competitors
whose platforms were gaining scale.

159
00:10:30,307 --> 00:10:34,856
BlackBerry understood mobile
communication better than almost anyone.

160
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:39,620
But understanding the old market
was not enough to win the new one.

161
00:10:41,215 --> 00:10:44,745
BlackBerry's downfall
reveals a dangerous paradox.

162
00:10:45,482 --> 00:10:50,056
The capabilities that make a company
successful can become the assumptions that

163
00:10:50,096 --> 00:10:51,286
limit its future.

164
00:10:51,775 --> 00:10:56,374
BlackBerry built a communications empire
around reliability, security, and

165
00:10:56,414 --> 00:10:57,414
productivity.

166
00:10:57,760 --> 00:10:59,478
Those strengths were real.

167
00:10:59,915 --> 00:11:04,126
But the smartphone became something
bigger than the product BlackBerry had

168
00:11:04,166 --> 00:11:05,166
perfected.

169
00:11:05,384 --> 00:11:08,197
The company survived by
changing its business.

170
00:11:08,844 --> 00:11:10,913
Its handset dominance did not.

171
00:11:11,367 --> 00:11:12,711
And that is the lesson.

172
00:11:13,211 --> 00:11:16,855
A market leader doesn't have to
stop innovating to fall behind.

173
00:11:17,341 --> 00:11:21,547
Sometimes it innovates in a direction
the market has already moved beyond.

174
00:11:22,155 --> 00:11:24,138
Every brand leaves evidence.

